// The boss journal: Volkar, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).volkar = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "Volkar never moved from the summit. He did not need to. Bad footing, wind from every side, and a charge of gold or blue on each of us. I set my feet anyway.",
    gloomsworn: "Volkar kept to his summit, wind coming from all sides and a gold or blue charge crawling on each of us. He never needed to come down. I have died to a blade; weather is slower.",
    thresher: "Met the weather on his summit. He never stirred from his field, only hung a charge of gold or blue on each of us, like tags on lambs. A farmer respects a storm. I took my hat off to him.",
    bloodreaver: "The big one is a mountain with lightning for blood. Every bolt left a mark, and the storm kept biting between them. I liked him at once.",
    runeguard: "The Titan writes his omens in the sky: cloud before the blast, sparks along the floor before the blow. A long text, but a legible one. I began a fresh page.",
    gorgemaw: "My host cooks on a mountaintop, in the rain, and every dish is lightning. Rustic. The storm never stops salting the table, and he never once leaves his kitchen.",
    oathbroker: "My counterparty is a mountain that does not move, and he binds each of us to a charge, gold or blue. The terms are long and the storm collects interest constantly. I read them twice.",
    ironpalm: "The mountain does not walk. It speaks from where it stands, and the storm murmurs under it, always. I listened a long time before I answered.",
    ranger: "Big quarry. Never moves. The weather does its running for it. Wind, rock, lightning, and a summit with no cover worth the name.",
    shadowblade: "The mark is a mountain who never turns round, because he never needs to. Lovely. The sky watches for him, though, and the sky has terrible manners.",
    pyromancer: "Volkar! A whole storm in one enormous body, and he outshone me. Lightning everywhere, wind tearing at my sleeves, rain on my best fire. I have never been so offended.",
    thermist: "The storm. Cold rain, hot lightning, never one long enough to settle. I ran both ways at once all evening. Back to the middle, I kept telling myself.",
    driftblade: "The giant never takes a step, but the wind runs for him, through and round and over all of us, and at last I have met something that moves as much as I do.",
    marionettist: "Our leading man never leaves centre stage; he more or less is the stage. The weather handles the scene changes. The company spent the first act learning its marks.",
    cleric: "Volkar barely moves. He needn't. The storm wears everyone down a little at a time and never stops. I prayed until my voice went. Dawn keep us on that mountain.",
    grovekeeper: "Old Thunder isn't a beast; he's weather with a face. Wind, rain and lightning, building all season long. My lot came down soaked and humbled.",
    chronomancer: "Volkar plays one long piece, the same every time, and it only grows louder. I'll have memorised it by the third hearing. I'm told I've heard it rather more than that.",
    alembist: "Ailment: Volkar. Symptoms: lightning, wind, rain in the reagents, and a storm grinding at everyone without pause. Remedy: still brewing. Half my shelf spoiled in the wet.",
    threadweaver: "The old windbag throws us apart and pulls us together all fight long, gold to blue. Every thread I tied, the wind tried to snap. My little stitches came home frayed.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "The sky came down and the line held it up together, every time, until he fell. Footing was bad to the last. Held.",
    gloomsworn: "The sky fell on us again and again, and we held it up between us until he went down. The mountain is quiet. I stood the whole way, and I did not even have to be dead for it.",
    thresher: "The weather broke at last and lay down on his own field, quiet as a cut row. We came down the mountain together, every hand of us. I said a few kind words over him. He was a good season, all told.",
    bloodreaver: "The big one went down at last, still crackling. I carried every bolt he gave me and kept every scar. My friend, you were magnificent.",
    runeguard: "The Titan's last omen was his own fall, and I had foreseen it for some while. The storm read to its final line. As foreseen, at last.",
    gorgemaw: "My host's last course was the sky itself, and we ate it together, all of us at one table. Then the kitchen fell silent. A long meal. Still hungry.",
    oathbroker: "All accounts with the counterparty are settled; the sky was paid for in common, to the last instalment. The storm defaulted first. Countersigned and closed.",
    ironpalm: "The mountain fell silent. The wind kept talking a while, then it too stopped. I breathed the clear air.",
    ranger: "The old stag went down in the rain. Never moved once, start to finish. Neither did my last arrow. Clean line.",
    shadowblade: "The mark crumbled into the clouds, still staring straight ahead. It never saw me, not once in all that weather. Delightful.",
    pyromancer: "I outshone the storm! Only at the very end, and only briefly, but I did. Volkar fell and the clouds parted. Just the one eyebrow lost.",
    thermist: "Ran hot to the last breath, then he fell and everything cooled at once. Rain on my face. Back to the middle, and finally still.",
    driftblade: "He fell and the wind finally dropped and I ran round the summit once more anyway, through the rain and out the other side, just because I could.",
    marionettist: "Curtain! Our leading man brought the house down, quite literally. The company held up the sky together for the finale. (The Doll bows; I accept the flowers.)",
    cleric: "Volkar fell, and every one of us walked down that mountain on our own feet. I have never prayed for so long. Thank the light.",
    grovekeeper: "The storm broke and the sky cleared, the way weather does if you outlast it. My lot stood together under the last of it. A good harvest after a long season.",
    chronomancer: "He fell at the end of his long piece, and for once I needn't hear it again. Or I did, many times, and this was the last. Either way.",
    alembist: "Ailment cured: Volkar. Remedy: patience, in many doses, and everyone together at the end. Shelf bare, workshop intact. One small fire, deliberate.",
    threadweaver: "The windbag fell and the threads held, every one, the whole hem pulled tight under the falling sky. Not a sloppy seam left on that mountain.",
  },
  moves: {
    'Thunderclap': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The clouds rolled over the summit and the light went. Then the clap reached every one of us. Nothing to block, so I braced and let the line be mended after. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Cloud rolled across the summit and the light drained out. Then the clap passed through all of us. There was no standing behind anything, so I let the healers do their work and let the dark take the rest.",
      thresher: "The clouds came over the summit and the light went, the way it does before a downpour. Then the clap came in on every one of us, no hedge to get under. I kept working and let the menders bring us round. Plenty of time.",
      bloodreaver: "The sky went dark, then cracked open across all of us. A decent sting! I took it and drank it back on the next few blows.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the summit darkens under rolling cloud. The answer: wounds closed beforehand, for the clap reaches all of us and cannot be stepped out of. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "When the clouds roll in, my host serves everyone the same plate at once. It cannot be sent back, only swallowed, so we came to table well fed beforehand. Bland, but filling.",
      oathbroker: "The clap is owed by all the undersigned at once, and no corner of the summit is exempt. The fine print: arrive whole, for it is paid on top of what the storm has already taken. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The clouds gathered. He spoke to all of us at once. There was nowhere to step, so I stood ready and let it pass through. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Sky went dark. Then the clap hit everyone, no lane out of it. Came in mended, kept shooting through it.",
      shadowblade: "The clouds rolled over and the mark clapped at the whole room. There is no hiding from that, so I simply made sure I was whole before it. Rude, but survivable.",
      pyromancer: "The whole sky went dark, the only time all evening anything was dimmer than me, and then it boomed across everyone at once! No dodging it. I'd been mended first. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Clouds. Dark. Then the clap, everywhere at once. I ran cold with dread and kept casting, because no corner of the summit escapes it. Whole beforehand is the only answer.",
      driftblade: "There is no route through the clap, it rolls over the whole summit at once, so I kept running my circles and made sure I was mended before the clouds closed overhead.",
      marionettist: "The house lights dimmed (clouds; a lovely effect) and the thunder struck the entire house. No wings to hide in, so the company simply arrived whole for its cue. A tolerable entr'acte.",
      cleric: "When the clouds roll over the summit, the clap is coming for everyone. I mended every wound before it rather than after, and nobody went down. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The sky darkens like a summer storm coming up the valley, then the clap reaches all of us. I'd planted in my lot as the clouds came over, so it was already mending when it struck. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "The clouds come a beat before the clap, and the clap reaches everyone. I'd had them all whole by the time the sky went dark, which was, or will be, just enough.",
      alembist: "Ailment: thunder across the whole summit, after the clouds roll in. Remedy: everyone topped up during the darkening, not after. Note: the storm's grind carries on beneath it.",
      threadweaver: "A clap across the whole hem at once. Nothing to dodge, so I had everyone mended and tied together before the clouds closed. It tugged every thread a little, and none snapped.",
    } },
    'Titan Fist': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Lightning gathered along his arm, pointed at me. I waited for the fist to drop, guard up in the last instant, and kept the line out of the path behind me. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Lightning crept down his arm toward me. I let the fist fall, drew the dark tight around me at the last, and kept the others out of the path behind. It does not stop at the first body.",
      thresher: "Lightning ran up his arm toward our knight, and I knew the fist was ripe. It falls down a long furrow, straight through the knight and out behind, so I worked from his flank, well off that row, and let it come in empty.",
      bloodreaver: "Lightning crawled down his arm and the fist came for me alone. A good one! I took it full, struck back while it still burned, and kept the others out from behind me, where it carries on.",
      runeguard: "The omen: lightning gathers along the line between the Titan and me. The answer: a ward laid just before, and nobody else in that line, for the blow runs on past me. On the harder climb it leaves me charged; I decline the next rod.",
      gorgemaw: "My host's signature dish, served to me alone down a long crackling table. I opened wide just as it landed, and made sure nobody sat behind me to share it. Spicy, I'll grant him.",
      oathbroker: "The fist is payable by whoever stands before him, and I accepted delivery with my guard raised. The fine print: the blow travels on past me, so no co-signatory may stand at my back. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Lightning gathered along his arm toward me. I raised my guard only at its last instant, and the others stood clear of the line behind me. It asked. I answered.",
      ranger: "Fist comes down a long lane, through our shield and out the other side. Kept to the flank, well off that line. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark's fist was for our shield, and the lightning ran on past him in a long line. I waited at the side, where neither fist nor eye could find me. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Lightning poured down his arm toward our knight and on past him, glowing the colour of trouble! I kept well to the side of that line and never stopped casting. Not a hair lost, for once.",
      thermist: "Lightning down his arm, toward our shield. Cold read: the blow runs on behind him, so not there. I stood to the side, burning, while it went past hot enough to taste.",
      driftblade: "His fist is for our shield, but the lightning carries on through him and out the far side, so I swung round to the flank, out of that lane, and back in the moment it passed.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man's big moment, aimed at our knight, and the blow carries on upstage behind him. I kept my Doll off that line, safely to the side. (The knight took his bow. Bruised, but upright.)",
      cleric: "When lightning gathers along his arm, the heavy blow is coming for our knight. I laid the light on him before it fell, and scolded the young archer for standing behind him. Dawn keep him.",
      grovekeeper: "Lightning creeps down his arm toward our shield like frost along a branch. I'd planted on the shield before it, and kept my lot off the line behind. It mended as it landed.",
      chronomancer: "The fist lands on the beat after his arm finishes glowing. I had our shield mended before then, and stood off the line behind him, where the blow keeps going. Nobody needed undoing. This time.",
      alembist: "Ailment: one huge blow to our shield, carrying on in a line behind him. Remedy: a dose already working on the shield beforehand, and nobody else in that line. Label it 'fist, his'.",
      threadweaver: "The fist went for our shield and ran on down the line behind him, so I kept my little stitches off that line and ran a thread to the shield to share the weight. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Polarity Surge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Each of us carries gold or blue. When the surge came I stood beside a blue one, close enough to touch, with no other gold near. Alone, it hits hard; beside my own colour, harder. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Gold or blue, each of us. When the surge came I sought the opposite colour, near enough to touch, and kept my own kind away. Two of a kind in one place is how the surge kills.",
      thresher: "Gold and blue, like wheat and cornflower. When the surge came due I stood close beside one of the other colour, near enough to share a hoe, and kept my own colour well off. Plant like beside like and the whole row scorches.",
      bloodreaver: "Gold beside gold makes a terrible arc; alone, a nasty bite. Gold beside blue, barely a tickle. Shameful! But I found my blue partner and stood close anyway.",
      runeguard: "The omen: arcs crackle between like charges before the surge, and a calm thread joins unlike ones. The answer: each stands beside an opposite charge, with no like charge in reach. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The surge is a pairing dish: gold with blue, never gold with gold, and never eaten alone at a table for one. I sat with my blue companion and it came out almost bland. Excellent pairing.",
      oathbroker: "The terms require each gold to be countersigned by a blue standing near. The fine print: a second gold within reach voids the contract and arcs through both. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Gold must stand with blue. Two golds argue, loudly. One alone shouts into the wind. I found my blue and we stood quietly together. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Paired up with a blue, shoulder to shoulder, nobody of my colour in reach. Other pair kept their distance.",
      shadowblade: "Gold must keep company with blue, never with its own kind and never alone. I stood beside my opposite, at a polite distance from anyone like me. A charming arrangement.",
      pyromancer: "I was gold that night, naturally. Standing near another gold made the air scream between us! So I stood close to a nice calm blue instead, and the surge barely tickled. Unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold sorted the pairs: blue beside me, gold kept away, nobody left alone. Then it surged, and the heat between us just fizzled out. Balance. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "I had to stand still beside my opposite, which I hate, so I circled until the arcs began to crackle and then landed right next to my blue, close enough to touch, well away from anyone gold.",
      marionettist: "A duet: each gold paired with a blue, close, and the duets kept apart. I took my place beside my partner on cue. (A charming bit of staging, if I say so.)",
      cleric: "Gold beside blue, near enough to hold hands, and no other gold close by. The young archer drifted toward her own colour once and arced dreadfully. Thank the light for second chances.",
      grovekeeper: "Gold and blue pair like rain and soil. My lot sorted themselves into couples, each gold rooted beside a blue, the couples apart. The surge passed over like a mild breeze.",
      chronomancer: "I found my opposite a beat before the surge, stood close, and kept my own colour out of reach. The arcs between like charges are the warning; I'll have heeded it every time since.",
      alembist: "Reagents: gold and blue. One of each, mixed close: inert. Two of a kind: violent. Alone: still bad. Remedy: everyone beside one opposite. Note: labelled clearly now.",
      threadweaver: "Gold tied to blue, close, a short thread between them, and no two golds in reach of each other. Anyone left alone took the whole surge. I tied them in pairs and kept the pairs apart.",
    } },
    'Thunder Crash': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A cloud gathered over each gold one of us. My blue partner and I stood together under mine and shared the blow; the other pair kept their distance. Then everyone broke apart for the static after. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Clouds hung over the gold ones. My blue partner and I took the one over mine and split the crash, while the other pair stood far off. When it passed we scattered from the static. Half a crash is survivable.",
      thresher: "A cloud ripened over each gold one of us. Each pair stood under their own cloud and took the rain together, the pairs a good row apart. Then the static came in behind it, so we broke off, every hand to their own ground.",
      bloodreaver: "A cloud over my partner's head. I stood right under it with her and took my half, a good meaty half, and then we scattered before the static came.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a storm cloud over every gold. The answer: each gold's blue partner stands beneath it with them to share the blow, the pairs well apart; then, at once, everyone scatters. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A cloud over each gold head: a sharing plate. My partner and I split ours down the middle, the other pair kept to their own table, and then we all scattered. Generous portion.",
      oathbroker: "The crash is drawn jointly: each gold with their blue, together under one cloud, each pair at a distance from the other. The fine print: the scatter follows immediately. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "A cloud over the gold. My partner and I stood together and took it as one. The other pair stood elsewhere. Then we parted, quickly.",
      ranger: "Clouds over the golds. Each pair stood together under theirs, pairs well apart. Then everyone scattered.",
      shadowblade: "A little cloud followed each gold one of us. I tucked in under mine with my partner, away from the other couple, and we slipped apart the instant it broke.",
      pyromancer: "A little thundercloud hung over my head like a bad mood! My blue partner stood under it with me, we split the crash, and then we all scattered before the static. Hair: frizzled.",
      thermist: "Cloud over my partner. Cold step in, under it with her, the other pair far off. Crash, shared. Then hot, fast, scatter.",
      driftblade: "A cloud over my partner meant landing beside her and staying there, which I endured, the other pair far across the summit, and the moment it broke I was off through the gap and away.",
      marionettist: "Scene: a cloud over each gold player. Blocking: each blue partner joins their gold beneath it, one pair upstage, one down. Then exeunt in every direction. A crisp little scene.",
      cleric: "The clouds gather over the gold ones. Each blue partner stood under with them, the two pairs apart, and they shared it. I mended both pairs before they scattered. Dawn keep them.",
      grovekeeper: "Clouds over the golds, like squalls over two fields. Each pair rooted together under their own, fields apart. Then everyone scattered like seed.",
      chronomancer: "Under the cloud in pairs, the pairs apart; then scatter a beat after it lands, because the static comes right on its heels. I keep hearing them as one bar of music.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a cloud over each gold, one heavy blow apiece. Remedy: each pair shares their own, the pairs kept apart. Note: scatter straight after; the second ailment is already steeping.",
      threadweaver: "Each gold had a cloud, so I tied each blue to their gold, close, and kept the pairs well apart. Then I cut every thread at once and they scattered. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Static Field': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Static settled on each of us at once. Every one of us needed our own patch of ground, away from the rest. I took mine and held it.",
      gloomsworn: "Static took hold of every one of us, and each needed room apart. I found my own bare place and stayed on it. A man who has been buried does not mind a small space.",
      thresher: "Static crept onto every head together. Each of us needed a patch with a gap round it, so nobody leaned on a neighbour. I wandered out to my corner and let it go over me.",
      bloodreaver: "A ring of static over every head. Mine alone, nobody else's in it. Barely a scratch, shameful.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a ring of static gathers over every head. The answer: each to their own ground, no ring touching another. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "Every guest got a little plate of static, and nobody should eat off their neighbour's. I kept to my own end of the table. Dull, but tidy.",
      oathbroker: "Each of the undersigned is liable for their own static, severally and not jointly. We stood apart, each settling our own account. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Static gathered over each of us. Each took their own ground, far from the others. Quiet. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Rings over every head. Fanned out, each to our own ground. Nobody touching.",
      shadowblade: "Static over every head, each ring wanting a patch of floor to itself. I took a quiet corner of my own, as ever. Solitude suits me.",
      pyromancer: "A crackling ring over my head, the colour of trouble, and one over everyone else's too! I took my own patch of the summit, far from anyone, and glowed in peace.",
      thermist: "Static over everyone. Clipped answer: apart. Each alone, no ring on another. Then breathe, warm up, close in again.",
      driftblade: "Static over every head, so I ran out to my own patch of the summit, nobody's ring touching mine, and the instant it snapped I was back through the middle again.",
      marionettist: "Everyone gets a solo. Each member of the company took their own spot on the stage, well apart. (Nobody upstaged anybody, for once.)",
      cleric: "Static gathered over every head. Each of the faithful took their own patch of ground, apart from the rest, and I mended them one by one after. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "A ring of static over each head. My lot spaced themselves like seedlings, each in their own bed, nobody's roots crossing. Then we gathered back up.",
      chronomancer: "Static over every head; I'll have learned by now that it follows the crash a beat later. Each of us to our own ground, far apart. Then together again.",
      alembist: "Ailment: static on each of us, which spills onto anyone near. Remedy: everyone apart, one apiece. Note: single doses afterwards.",
      threadweaver: "Each of my little stitches carried a ring of static, and those must not overlap. I let out all the slack, everyone apart, then drew them back in once it cleared.",
    } },
    'Rod Strike': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A ring burned down over my head. I put my back to the nearest standing stone and the bolt struck the rock, not me. The next pair took the other stones; a struck stone needs time. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The ring sat on my head like a crown. I went to the nearest stone, turned my back to it, and the lightning went into the stone. Next pair, different stones; a stone struck once needs a rest. Not unlike me.",
      thresher: "A ring settled on my head, and I took it the bolt was close. I backed against the nearest standing stone and the lightning went into it. The next pair used different stones; a struck one wants resting, same as a field.",
      bloodreaver: "Marked! I ran to a standing stone and the bolt hit the rock instead of me. A pity. The next pair went to different stones; strike one twice in a hurry and it bursts on everyone near.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a burning ring over a head. The answer: that person stands against a standing stone, and the bolt goes into the rock; the second pair choose untouched stones, for a freshly struck one discharges. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A bolt meant for me, so I carried it to a standing stone and let the rock be the plate. The second pair used fresh stones; a used one spits back at the table. Once, mouth open, I simply swallowed mine.",
      oathbroker: "Marked, I conveyed the bolt to a standing stone, which assumed the debt. The fine print: a stone lately struck cannot bear a second, so the next pair must use the others. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The ring burned over my head. I walked to a stone and stood against it. The sky spoke to the rock, not to me. The next two found other stones.",
      ranger: "Marked. Ran to the nearest standing stone, shoulder to it. Bolt took the rock. Second pair went for the other stones, not ours.",
      shadowblade: "A ring over my head: the sky had noticed me, how rude. I leant on a standing stone and let it take the bolt. The next pair chose fresh stones; a struck one sulks and lashes out.",
      pyromancer: "A ring burned down over my head! I dashed to a standing stone, hugged it, and the bolt struck the rock instead of my lovely hair. The next pair went to the other stones, sensibly.",
      thermist: "Marked: a ring burning down. Cold: nearest stone, back against it, others away. Bolt into the rock, hot through my teeth. Next pair, fresh stones. Never the same one twice.",
      driftblade: "A ring burning down over my head meant run, for once, straight to a standing stone and stop against it so the bolt struck the rock, and the next pair ran past us to the other stones.",
      marionettist: "Cue: a ring over two heads. Those players each take a standing stone and stand against it; the bolt plays to the rock. The second pair take fresh stones. (The rest of the company keeps clear.)",
      cleric: "Two of the faithful were marked, and each took their bolt against a standing stone. The next pair took the others, for a stone struck twice so soon bursts on everyone near. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "A ring over a head is lightning looking for a tall tree. My marked ones stood against the standing stones and let the stone take it; the next pair chose fresh stones. Never strike the same tree twice.",
      chronomancer: "Two marked, then two more a beat later. Each goes to a stone and lets the bolt find it; the second pair uses the stones the first left alone, since a struck stone remembers. I'll have remembered too.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a bolt marked for one person. Remedy: stand against a standing stone; the rock takes it. Note: a used stone stays charged a while; the second pair uses fresh ones or it spoils for everyone near.",
      threadweaver: "The marked ones walked to a stone each and stood right against it. The next pair went to other stones. Everyone else kept their hem well clear of the struck rocks.",
    } },
    'Gale Slam': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He drew breath and the wind threw us all outward. Where a rod stood, I stepped into the still air behind it, away from him. With no rods, I stood close in so the throw stopped short of rock. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "He sucked in a breath and the wind threw us outward. If a rod was nearby I sheltered in the calm behind it, the side away from him; if none, I closed in beside him, so I flew only a short way.",
      thresher: "He drew breath and flung us all outward like chaff off a threshing floor. A rod nearby meant I tucked in on its far side from him; with no rods, I stood right at his knees, so the throw ran out before the rocks.",
      bloodreaver: "The big one breathed and threw us at the walls. Slammed into stone? Not even an honest wound. So: behind a standing rod, out of the wind, or close in, where the throw lands on open ground.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the Titan draws breath. The answer: the still air behind a standing rod, on its far side from him; or, with no rods, near enough to him that the throw ends short of wall and stone. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A dish made of wind alone, and it hurls the diner across the room. One eats it in the lee of a standing rod; with no rods, close to my host, so one lands nowhere hard. All air, no substance.",
      oathbroker: "The gale's terms: everyone thrown outward, and whoever is flung into stone pays dearly. The exemption clause: the still air behind a planted rod. Failing rods, stand close enough to him that the throw ends on open floor.",
      ironpalm: "He breathed in. I stood in the stillness behind a rod, and the wind went past. When no rod stood, I stepped close to him, and landed lightly. Breathe.",
      ranger: "He breathes, the wind throws you outward. In a rod's lee, you don't move. No rods: stand in close, so the throw runs out before the rocks.",
      shadowblade: "He took a great breath and flung everyone away. I tucked into the lee behind a rod, like a cat behind a curtain. No rods? Stand close to him and land softly.",
      pyromancer: "He inhaled like a bellows and hurled us all outward! I hid in the still air behind a standing rod and kept casting. With no rods up, I stood close so I landed on open floor, not rock.",
      thermist: "He drew breath. Cold: behind a rod, in its quiet strip, on the side away from him. Or close in when there are none, so the throw falls short of stone. Then the wind, hot and enormous, and I didn't move.",
      driftblade: "He breathed and the wind threw everyone and I wanted to fly with it, truly, but the rocks argued, so I tucked in behind a standing rod, out of his breath, and with no rods I went in close so I'd land short.",
      marionettist: "Stage direction: the leading man inhales; the company is blown into the scenery. Better blocking: stand behind a planted rod, in its still air. With no rods, stand close to him, so the throw stops before the flats.",
      cleric: "He drew a great breath and the wind threw us outward. The faithful sheltered behind a standing rod, away from him. When none stood, we kept close to him, so nobody was dashed against stone. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "The gale came on like a spring blow off the peaks. My lot sheltered in the lee behind the rods, like seedlings behind a wall. With no rods, we stood close in, so the wind's throw fell short.",
      chronomancer: "He breathes in, and a beat later the wind throws everyone outward. Behind a standing rod, nothing moves you; with no rods, close to him, the throw ends before the rocks. I've been thrown enough times to know.",
      alembist: "Ailment: everyone thrown outward, hard into any rock. Remedy: shelter behind a standing rod, on the side away from him. Note: no rods, stand close to him, so the landing is open floor.",
      threadweaver: "When he breathed, I drew everyone in behind the standing rods, where the air was still. With no rods, I pulled the hem in close around him, so the throw ended before the rocks. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Storm Walls': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Walls of wind turned slowly round him like spokes. I kept my place between them and walked with them, step for step. Where a boulder lay, I stood behind it. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Walls of wind swung around him slowly, like cartwheel spokes. I found the gap and shuffled round with it. A boulder nearby was something to put between me and the wall.",
      thresher: "Walls of wind wheeled round him slowly, like a mill wheel. I found the gap between two spokes and walked along with it, unhurried; a boulder, once, made a fine bit of shelter.",
      bloodreaver: "Walls of wind wheeling round the big one. Stood in the gap and walked with it, hitting him the whole way round. A wall would sting, but walking is free.",
      runeguard: "The omen: lines of wind drawn outward from the Titan. The answer: stand in the gap between them and walk with the turning, never against it; a boulder's lee holds, once. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host's revolving dessert trolley: walls of wind, turning. I walked with the gap between them, never into them, and sheltered behind a boulder when one had been laid. Overwhipped, yet refreshing.",
      oathbroker: "The walls revolve on fixed terms: slowly, and in one direction. We walked within the gap at their pace. The fine print: a boulder shelters from a single pass, and is then spent.",
      ironpalm: "Walls of wind turned around him. I walked with them, in the space between. Slow steps. When a boulder lay there, I let it speak for me.",
      ranger: "Walls turn round it like a mill wheel. Pick a gap, walk with it. Boulder's lee works, once.",
      shadowblade: "Walls of wind wheeling round the mark. I walked with them in the gap, like a shadow across a sundial, keeping pace. A boulder hid me, once. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Turning walls of wind! I had to walk while casting, which is undignified, keeping to the gap between them as they swept round. Behind a boulder I could stand still, gloriously, once.",
      thermist: "Walls, turning. Cold: find the gap, walk with it, keep pace. Too fast, the wall ahead; too slow, the one behind. Back to the middle of the gap.",
      driftblade: "Walls of wind turning round him are only a route that moves, so I ran round with them inside the gap, never through a wall, and behind a boulder when there was one.",
      marionettist: "A revolving stage! The company walked with it, between the walls, and my Doll kept her place in the gap. Boulders made a fine screen, for one turn. (Reviewers praised the choreography.)",
      cleric: "The walls of wind turned slowly round him. The faithful walked with them in the gaps, and those behind boulders were sheltered once. I mended as we walked. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The walls turn like a slow wind round a hill. My lot walked with them, in the gaps, at the walls' own pace. A boulder gave shelter for one season.",
      chronomancer: "The walls turn at an even tempo, and walking with them in the gap is like keeping time. A boulder shelters for one pass, then it's gone. I'll have walked the whole circle by now.",
      alembist: "Ailment: walls of wind turning slowly round him. Remedy: walk in the gap between, at their pace. Note: a boulder shelters from one pass, then spoils.",
      threadweaver: "The walls turned round him and I kept my little stitches in the gap, walking together, nobody lagging into the wall behind. A boulder covered us once. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Chain Lightning': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "A bolt gathered over our healer while his fist came at me. She walked away from the line, and the rest of us stood well apart, so it had nobody to leap to. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "A bolt gathered over our healer as his fist came for me. She walked away from everyone and the others kept apart, so it had nothing to jump to. I took the fist myself; it was mine to take.",
      thresher: "A bolt gathered over our healer while his fist fell on the knight. She strode away alone, and the rest of us spaced out like stooks, so the lightning had nowhere to jump.",
      bloodreaver: "Lightning over our healer's head, itching to jump. She walked off alone and I kept my distance from her and everyone. Hard not to share, but every leap hurts someone new.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a cell gathers over one of us, ringed by how far it can leap. The answer: that one walks clear of everyone, and the rest stand apart from each other besides. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host served a dish that hops from plate to plate. Its first diner walked away from the table; the rest of us sat far apart. Once, mouth open, I let it hop into me and end there. Still hungry.",
      oathbroker: "A bolt bound to one of the undersigned, which passes to whoever stands nearest. The fine print: everyone outside its reach, and apart from one another, and the debt goes no further.",
      ironpalm: "Lightning gathered over our healer. She walked away. The rest of us kept our distance, each alone. The bolt had no one to talk to.",
      ranger: "Storm over the healer. She walked off; the rest of us kept our own distance. Bolt had nowhere to jump.",
      shadowblade: "A storm cell crept over our healer. She drifted off alone, gracefully, and the rest of us kept our distance from her and from each other. Lightning cannot leap to a shadow.",
      pyromancer: "A storm cloud over our healer, with a ring showing how far it could leap! She walked off on her own, and we all kept away from her and from each other. I stood at the very edge, nobly.",
      thermist: "Bolt over our healer. Cold: everyone out of her ring, and out of each other's. She walked alone. It struck her and found nobody else.",
      driftblade: "A bolt gathered over our healer so I ran wide, out past the ring that showed how far it leaps, nobody else near me either, and it struck her and found no path through the rest of us.",
      marionettist: "A solo for our healer: the bolt hangs over her, and the company leaves her the stage, everyone far apart. (My Doll waited in the wings.)",
      cleric: "A bolt gathered over me just as the fist fell on our knight. I walked away from everyone and asked them to keep apart. It struck me once and stopped. Thank the light it was me and not the young ones.",
      grovekeeper: "A cloud over my own head, building like a thunderhead on a hot day. I walked off from my lot, they kept apart from each other, and it struck me alone. Plant first; I'd mended myself already.",
      chronomancer: "The bolt gathers over me as the fist lands on our shield; two beats at once. I walk off from everyone, the others keep apart, and it strikes only me. I'll have mended the shield on the way.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a bolt over me, wanting to leap. Remedy: walk away from everyone; the others keep apart too. Note: dose the shield before walking, since his fist lands at the same moment.",
      threadweaver: "The bolt gathered over me, so I cut every thread and walked away, and my little stitches kept apart from each other. It struck once and found nothing to run along.",
    } },
    'Lightning Rods': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Iron rods driven into the rock, each wanting one of us when the bolt fell. I took one; I did not take the next while still crackling from the first. The rods stood after, good cover. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Iron rods were set in the stone, each hungry for one of us when the bolt came. I took one, then sat out the next while I still crackled. They stood on after, as shelter.",
      thresher: "Iron rods stood in the stone like fence posts, each waiting on one of us for the bolt. I took a rod, then let someone else have the next while I still crackled. Good shelter afterwards, those posts.",
      bloodreaver: "A rod for each of us to catch. I took one and the bolt went straight through me. Lovely. The next rod I left alone; twice running would floor even me. Probably.",
      runeguard: "The omen: iron rods driven into the rock. The answer: one of us in each before the bolt falls; anyone still charged from a rod stands aside for the next. An empty rod strikes us all. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "Rods of iron, one per guest, each serving a single bolt. I took mine. A second helping while still buzzing from the first is poison, so I left that course to others. No rod goes uneaten.",
      oathbroker: "Each rod must be answered by exactly one of the undersigned. The fine print: whoever caught the last one is still charged and must not sign again. An unanswered rod defaults on everyone.",
      ironpalm: "Rods struck the rock. One of us stood in each. The one who answered last time rested. Afterwards the rods remained, quiet shelter from the wind.",
      ranger: "Rods went in. One of us to each. Whoever caught the last one sits out. Rods stay standing; useful cover after.",
      shadowblade: "Iron rods, each wanting a body when the bolt falls. I took one, still and patient, then let someone fresh take the next. Leave one empty and it strikes us all, which would be vulgar.",
      pyromancer: "Iron rods hammered into the summit, each begging for a lightning bolt, and of course I volunteered! Next time I stood aside, still fizzing. One bolt in a while is plenty, even for me.",
      thermist: "Rods. One each, nobody still charged from the last. Cold rule. The bolt hit and I ran hot right through, then let the next rod go to someone cooler.",
      driftblade: "A rod for each of us meant standing in one, still, until the bolt came through me, then letting somebody fresh take the next, and afterwards the rods stood there as shelter from his breath.",
      marionettist: "Casting call: one member of the company per rod, never whoever played the last one. An empty rod brings the house down on us all. (The rods stayed on as scenery, and very useful scenery.)",
      cleric: "The rods wanted one of us each. Our brave knight took one, and I steered him off the next, for he still crackled from the first. Nobody left one empty. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Rods driven in like stakes for beans. One of my lot to each stake, and whoever caught the last one rests a season. Afterwards the stakes stood as windbreaks.",
      chronomancer: "One of us to each rod, and whoever caught the last one sits this one out, still humming. I'll have said so every time. An empty rod strikes everyone; a rod caught twice running strikes one person very hard.",
      alembist: "Ailment: rods that want one person each. Remedy: one fresh person per rod. Note: anyone still charged from the last spoils if dosed again; keep them off it. The rods stay standing, shelter for later.",
      threadweaver: "One rod, one stitch: I sent a person to each, and kept the one still humming from the last away from the new ones. Nobody left a rod empty. The rods made good pins afterwards.",
    } },
    'Avalanche': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He stamped and boulders rolled across the summit in lanes. I stepped out of the lanes before they came and let them settle. Then I stood behind one when the walls came round. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Boulders came down the summit in lanes after his stamp. I waited between the lanes, then took the lee of a stone for the wind walls that follow.",
      thresher: "He stamped and the boulders came down in rows, like a cart tipping its load. I stood in the furrow between the rows and let them roll past. Once they settled, I kept one at my back for when the wind walls came round.",
      bloodreaver: "He stomped and rocks came rolling down marked lanes. Flattened by a rock? No honour in that. Stepped out of the lane, then hid behind the boulder when the walls came.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the stamp, and lanes marked across the summit. The answer: stand between the lanes as the boulders roll; then behind the boulders, for the walls of wind that follow. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "He stamped and the boulders came rolling down the table in lanes. One does not eat rocks. I stepped between the lanes, then sat behind a boulder when the walls of wind came round. Far too dry.",
      oathbroker: "The stamp gives notice: boulders along marked lanes. We stood between the lanes. The fine print: once at rest, each boulder shelters one from the walls of wind that follow, but only once.",
      ironpalm: "He stamped. Stones rolled down their paths. I stood where no path ran. Then I waited behind a stone for the wind.",
      ranger: "He stamped. Boulders rolled in lanes from one side. Stood between the lanes. Then behind a boulder for the walls.",
      shadowblade: "He stamped and boulders rolled across in neat lanes. I stood between them, then slipped behind one once it stopped, and the walls of wind passed right over me. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "He stamped, and rocks came rolling across the summit like a dreadful parade! I stepped out of their lanes, then hid behind one when the walls of wind came round. A rock makes a fine parasol.",
      thermist: "Stamp. Lanes on the floor. Cold: stand between them. Then rocks, rolling, roaring, and I ran hot until they stopped, and slid behind one for the wind.",
      driftblade: "He stamped and boulders came rolling down their lanes and I slipped out of their path and between them, and as they settled I ducked behind one so the walls of wind went over me.",
      marionettist: "Scene change: boulders roll on from the wings in marked lanes. The company stands between the lanes, then hides behind the new scenery when the walls of wind come round. (Props department: flawless.)",
      cleric: "He stamped and the boulders rolled in along their lanes. The faithful stood clear of the lanes, then sheltered behind the stones when the walls of wind came. Thank the light for rocks.",
      grovekeeper: "He stamped like a rockslide starting on a hillside, and the boulders came down their lanes. My lot stood between them. Then we sheltered behind them when the wind came round.",
      chronomancer: "He stamps, the boulders roll a beat later, and the walls of wind follow on their heels. Between the lanes, then behind a boulder. I'll have learned the whole phrase by heart.",
      alembist: "Ailment: boulders rolling down marked lanes. Remedy: stand between the lanes. Note: the boulders then shelter from the walls of wind, once each. Use them before they spoil.",
      threadweaver: "He stamped, and I pulled my little stitches out of the boulders' lanes. When they settled, we tucked in behind them for the walls of wind. A neat seam.",
    } },
    'Squall Line': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The storm's edge closed on one side and opened on the other, and the safe ground walked slowly round him. The line walked with it, step for step, never outside. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The storm closed on one side and opened on the other, so the dry place travelled slowly round him. We walked with it, all of us, and none stepped out into the rain.",
      thresher: "The storm's edge shut on one side and opened on the other, so the dry patch crept around him like shade round a barn. We went with it together and never stepped out into the rain.",
      bloodreaver: "The safe ground crawled round the big one and outside it the storm bit every heartbeat. Tempting, but no. Walked round with it and kept hitting him.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the safe ground slides off its centre, with a ghost of where it goes next. The answer: walk with it round the Titan, inside its edge, and keep striking; he is always within it. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The table itself moved round my host, and past its edge the storm served nothing but sparks. I walked with the table, eating as I went. Inconvenient service, but I finished my plate.",
      oathbroker: "The safe ground moves round him on schedule, and we kept to its boundary as it went. The fine print: the storm outside charges for every moment one lingers. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The safe ground turned around him. I walked with it, still striking. The storm outside spoke without pause. I did not listen.",
      ranger: "Safe ground drifted round it. Walked with it, kept my shots coming. Always inside the edge.",
      shadowblade: "The safe ground drifted round the mark like a slow dance, and outside it the storm was cruel. I strolled along inside, still at his back. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "The safe ground wandered off round him, so I had to walk while casting, which I despise! I kept inside its edge the whole way round and kept the fire coming. Outside, it glowed the colour of trouble.",
      thermist: "The safe ring drifting round him. Cold: walk with it, inside the edge, watch the ghost ahead. Outside, the storm was scalding. I kept to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The safe ground circled round him slowly, finally a fight that moves, and I ran round with it inside its edge, a step ahead of where it was going, never out in the storm, never landed.",
      marionettist: "The stage itself revolved round our leading man, and the wings were full of storm. The company walked with it, staying in the light, playing on. (The Doll never missed a step.)",
      cleric: "The safe ground drifted round him and the storm raged at its edges. I kept the faithful inside it, walking together, and called back anyone who strayed. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The calm patch moves round him like the shade of a tree through a summer day. My lot followed it round, inside its edge. Stray out of the shade and the weather bites.",
      chronomancer: "The safe ground circles him once, slowly, and you walk with it, inside the edge, the whole way. I could see where it would be a moment before it got there. I usually was.",
      alembist: "Ailment: storm everywhere but a circle that travels slowly round him. Remedy: walk with it, inside the edge, and keep working. Note: everything else in that stretch happens inside it too.",
      threadweaver: "The safe ground slid round him, and I herded my little stitches along inside it, nobody straggling at the edge. A slow walk, and a tidy one.",
    } },
    'Ball Lightning': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Orbs of lightning formed at the cliff edge and drifted slowly across the summit. I watched where each was heading and stepped off its path. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Lightning orbs rose along the cliff and wandered across the summit. I marked each one's bearing and kept out of its way. Slow does not mean harmless.",
      thresher: "Orbs of lightning came off the cliff's edge and drifted across the summit, slow as thistledown, each down its own straight row. I watched which way each was heading, stepped off its line, and went back to work. Plenty of time.",
      bloodreaver: "Balls of lightning wandering across the summit. Touch one and it bursts and flings you. I stepped aside. Not every scar is worth having.",
      runeguard: "The omen: orbs born of falling bolts at the cliff edge, each marked with its heading. The answer: read every path and stand clear of all of them as they drift through. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "Little floating morsels of lightning, drifting across the table. They burst in the mouth, and not pleasantly. I stepped aside of each one's path. Fizzy, and best left on the tray.",
      oathbroker: "The orbs travel fixed courses at a slow pace, declared in advance by the marks before them. We stood off every route. The fine print: they come staggered, not all together.",
      ironpalm: "Orbs drifted across the summit. Slowly. I watched their paths and stepped out of them. They passed without a word.",
      ranger: "Orbs drift in from the edge on straight lines. Read the heading, step off the line. Easy shot past them.",
      shadowblade: "Crackling orbs wandered over the summit, staggered and slow. I stepped politely out of each one's way. They burst on whoever they touch, and I am not one to be touched.",
      pyromancer: "Floating balls of lightning, very nearly as bright as me! I watched where each was heading and stepped out of its way rather than argue. Eyebrows intact.",
      thermist: "Orbs, slow, staggered. Cold: read the heading, then step off it. They drifted past, hot and humming.",
      driftblade: "Orbs drifted in from the cliff edge, slow and dull, and I ran round each one, through the gaps between them, never into one, and wished they'd hurry up.",
      marionettist: "Glowing extras wander across the stage, slowly, on fixed paths. The company steps out of their blocking; my Doll stepped round them with real poise.",
      cleric: "Orbs of lightning drifted across the summit from the cliff edge. I warned the faithful of their paths, and each stepped aside. The young archer nearly bumped one. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Orbs drifted across like dandelion seed on a slow wind. My lot watched where each was blowing and stepped out of its way.",
      chronomancer: "The orbs come one after another, a beat apart, and slow. Read each heading and step off its path. I'll have stepped aside in good time.",
      alembist: "Ailment: drifting orbs that burst on contact. Remedy: read their heading and stand off their paths. Note: they arrive staggered; don't relax after the first.",
      threadweaver: "Orbs drifted slowly across, and I nudged my little stitches out of their paths, one at a time. Nobody snagged on one.",
    } },
    'Eye of the Storm': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The wind dropped and the sky cleared. Nothing struck. I kept on him, kept the line together, and watched for what came next. On the harder climb our colours turn here; I checked mine. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The wind fell away. The sky opened. Nothing came. I kept at him and waited for the next thing. On the harder climbs our colours flip in this calm, and I checked mine.",
      thresher: "Wind dropped, sky cleared, nothing fell: a suspicious sort of quiet. I kept working and kept looking. On the harder climb the colours swap over here, so I checked mine.",
      bloodreaver: "The storm stopped! No more biting from the sky. Too quiet. I kept hitting him, and on the hard climb I checked my colour twice; it flips when the calm comes.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the clouds part and the storm's steady grind ceases. The answer: rest, mend, strike; and on the harder ascent, read one's charge anew, for it reverses. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A palate cleanser: the storm stops, the air goes still. My host stays at table, so I kept eating. On the harder climb, everyone's colour turns over, like a crêpe.",
      oathbroker: "A grace period: the storm ceases charging, the sky clears, and he stays open to us. The fine print, on the harder climb: every charge reverses as it opens. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The wind stopped. The storm fell silent. He did not. I kept speaking to him in the quiet. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Wind dropped. Sky opened. Quarry still there. Kept shooting. On the hard climb, colours swap here; check yours.",
      shadowblade: "The storm stopped and the sky opened, very civil of it. The mark stayed put, so I kept working. On the harder climb our colours swap here; I glanced down to see which I'd become.",
      pyromancer: "The clouds parted, the wind died, and for once I could stand still and burn! He stays open to it. Mind, on the harder climb our colours flip here, so I checked mine.",
      thermist: "The wind stopped. Cold, still, clear. I breathed. Then, on the hard climb: the colours turn over. Check yours before walking anywhere.",
      driftblade: "The wind dropped, which I hated, everything so still, so I kept circling him anyway, cutting through the quiet, and on the hard climb checked my colour, which flips here.",
      marionettist: "Intermission, of a kind. The storm hushes, the sky clears, and our leading man stays onstage, so the company plays on. (On the harder run, everyone's costume changes colour.)",
      cleric: "The clouds parted and the storm stopped wearing at us. I used the quiet to mend everyone fully. On the harder climb the colours turn over here; I reminded the faithful to look.",
      grovekeeper: "The eye came like a still afternoon in the middle of a gale. I mended my lot while the weather held off, and on the harder climb checked whose colour had turned.",
      chronomancer: "The storm stops, long enough to mend everyone: the rest before the second movement. On the harder climb the colours swap as it opens; I'll have noticed.",
      alembist: "Ailment: none, briefly. The storm stops. Remedy: restock everyone while it's quiet. Note: on the harder climb, charges reverse here; relabel.",
      threadweaver: "The wind dropped and I used the quiet to mend every frayed thread. On the harder climb everyone's colour flips here, so I re-tied the pairs.",
    } },
    'Still Air': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The standing stones lit, gold and blue. I went to a stone of my own colour, alone at it, and held that ground until the eye closed. Every stone had one of us. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The stones lit, gold and blue. I took the one that matched me, stood by it alone, and waited for the eye to shut. Not one stone was left empty.",
      thresher: "The standing stones lit in gold and blue. I took the one that matched me and stood by it alone till the calm passed. Every stone had somebody; nothing left bare.",
      bloodreaver: "Stones lit up gold and blue. Found one of my own colour and stood at it alone. Wrong colour, it strikes you. Empty stone, it strikes everyone.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the stones kindle, gold and blue, opposite stones alike. The answer: each to a stone of their own charge, one apiece, there before the Eye closes; none left empty. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The stones lit like places set at table, gold and blue, one per guest. I sat at a place of my own colour. Sit at the wrong one and it bites; leave one empty and it throws a tantrum at everyone.",
      oathbroker: "Each lit stone requires one signatory of matching charge. We assigned ourselves accordingly, one apiece. The fine print: a stone left unanswered discharges across the whole summit.",
      ironpalm: "The stones lit, gold and blue. I went to one of my colour and waited there. Each of us at our own. Every stone was answered.",
      ranger: "Stones lit gold and blue. Took one of my colour, alone. Every stone covered.",
      shadowblade: "The stones lit up gold and blue, and each of us claimed one of our own colour, alone. A wrong colour gets struck; an empty stone punishes everyone. I took mine early and waited, unseen.",
      pyromancer: "The stones glowed gold and blue, and each of us took one in our own colour, one apiece! Standing still beside something glowing is my natural state. Leave one empty and everyone suffers.",
      thermist: "Stones lit. Gold, blue, gold, blue. Cold: my colour, alone, before the calm ends. Others to theirs. Every stone taken. Then I could run warm.",
      driftblade: "The stones lit gold and blue and I ran to one of my own colour, nobody else's, one of us to each stone, and then I had to stand there until the calm ended, which was agony.",
      marionettist: "The marks lit up on the stage, gold and blue; each member of the company to a mark of their own colour, one apiece, none left bare. (A rare moment of perfect blocking.)",
      cleric: "The stones lit, gold and blue. Each of the faithful went to one of their own colour, one to a stone, and none was left empty. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The stones lit like lanterns at harvest, gold and blue. Each of my lot went to one of their own colour, one apiece, and every stone was tended.",
      chronomancer: "The stones light early; the calm closes much later. Each of us to a stone of our own colour, one apiece, and there before the end. I'll have been there early.",
      alembist: "Ailment: lit stones, gold and blue, each wanting one person. Remedy: each to a stone of their own colour. Note: wrong colour spoils the dose; an empty stone spoils everyone.",
      threadweaver: "The stones lit gold and blue, and I sent each little stitch to a stone of their own colour, one apiece. Nobody doubled up, and none stood empty.",
    } },
    'Distant Thunder': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Thunder rolled, far off. Nothing reached us. I kept my ground at my stone and did not flinch. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Thunder rolled somewhere far away and nothing touched us. I stayed beside my stone and did not flinch. Standing still is one thing the dead do well.",
      thresher: "Thunder rolled away over the far hills, somebody else's weather. Nothing came in on us. I stayed at my stone and did not go wandering off to look. Not yet.",
      bloodreaver: "Thunder, way off over the peaks. Not a scratch! Shameful. I kept swinging.",
      runeguard: "The omen: thunder, far off. The answer: none needed; it is only the storm gathering beyond the eye. A sign without a blow. Not foreseen; now it is.",
      gorgemaw: "Thunder rumbled in the distance, like a kitchen clattering in another building. Nothing was served. I kept eating.",
      oathbroker: "Distant thunder: notice of future terms, with no present liability. We kept to our stones and our work. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Thunder far off. It was not speaking to us. I kept my breath.",
      ranger: "Thunder over the far peaks. Nothing landed. Kept shooting.",
      shadowblade: "Thunder rumbled, far away, and nothing came of it. All bluster. I kept to my work.",
      pyromancer: "A great rumble of thunder, and then nothing at all. Theatrical! I approve. I kept casting.",
      thermist: "Thunder, far off. Cold check: nothing landing. Kept still. Kept working.",
      driftblade: "Thunder rolled somewhere far off and nothing came of it, so I kept circling, through the quiet and round past the stones.",
      marionettist: "Offstage thunder (sound effects only). The company ignored it and kept to their marks. A fine bit of atmosphere.",
      cleric: "Thunder rumbled beyond the clouds, but nothing fell on us. I kept mending in the quiet. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Thunder in the next valley. Nothing for us. I kept planting.",
      chronomancer: "Thunder from far off, all sound and no strike. A rest in the music. I'll have spent it well.",
      alembist: "Ailment: none. Distant thunder; nothing lands. Remedy: keep brewing.",
      threadweaver: "Thunder far off, and nothing came of it. I kept stitching.",
    } },
    'Eye Closes': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The calm ended. I was at my stone, my own colour, when it did, and the storm came back down on the line. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "At my stone, in my colour, when the calm ended. Then the storm returned, and I stood in it as I stand in everything.",
      thresher: "The calm ended the way a fine afternoon turns. By then I was by my stone, my own colour, and the weather came straight back.",
      bloodreaver: "The quiet broke. I was at a stone of my own colour when it did, as I should be. Then the sky came back, biting. Good.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the calm draws shut. The answer: be at one's stone, of one's own charge, before it closes; then brace for the storm resumed. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The calm closed and the kitchen roared back to life. I was seated at my own place, the right colour. Then the next course.",
      oathbroker: "The grace period expires as the eye closes; anyone not at a stone of matching charge defaults. I was at mine. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "The calm closed. I was at my stone. The storm spoke again.",
      ranger: "Calm closed. At my stone, right colour. Storm came back down.",
      shadowblade: "The calm ended, rather abruptly. I was already at my stone, my own colour, quietly waiting. Then back to the mark's back.",
      pyromancer: "The sky shut like a door! I was already at a stone of my own colour, posed beautifully. Then the storm resumed, and so did I.",
      thermist: "Eye closing. Cold: at my stone, my colour. Then hot again: the storm back, and all of us moving.",
      driftblade: "The calm closed while I was at my stone, my colour, which meant landing for once, and then the storm came back and I could finally move again.",
      marionettist: "Curtain on the interlude. Each member of the company on their mark, their own colour. Then the storm returns for the second act.",
      cleric: "The calm closed. Each of the faithful stood at a stone of their own colour when it did. Then the storm returned, and I returned to my prayers.",
      grovekeeper: "The calm ends the way a fine mountain day ends: all at once. My lot were at their stones, right colour each. Then weather again.",
      chronomancer: "The calm closes on the beat, and you're at your stone by then or you're too late. I was. Probably. Then the second half begins.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the calm ending. Remedy: be at your stone, own colour, before it shuts. Note: the storm resumes at once.",
      threadweaver: "The calm closed with every little stitch at its own stone, right colour. Then I drew them in for the storm.",
    } },
    'Gathering Storm': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Static settled on all of us and bit, over and over. I kept close to the healers, guard ready, and let them mend the line as it went. Held, by a thread.",
      gloomsworn: "Static settled on all of us and kept biting. I stayed close to the healers so they could keep us mended. Held up by a thread, and I know the sensation.",
      thresher: "Static settled on all of us and stung, over and over, like nettles in a hedge. I kept close by the menders so they could tend the whole row at once, and kept working while it lasted. Soft ground, but we stood on it.",
      bloodreaver: "The storm gathered in all of us at once, biting again and again, more than any of us could carry alone! I kept hitting to drink it back, and the healers did the rest.",
      runeguard: "The omen: static clings to every one of us. The answer: gather near the healers, keep every ward in hand, and expect the Thunderhead behind it. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A long tasting menu: every guest served static, over and over, and a great dish to finish. Too much for anyone to eat alone, so we sat together by the healers and they kept us topped up.",
      oathbroker: "The storm accrues interest on all of us at once, more than any single account can bear. We paid in instalments, gathered near the healers, our guards held in reserve for the final sum.",
      ironpalm: "Static gathered on everyone and spoke many times. We stood close to the healers. We breathed through it.",
      ranger: "Static on everyone, again and again. Stayed near the healers. Kept shooting.",
      shadowblade: "Static clung to all of us and stung, again and again. Even I came out of the shadows to stand near our healers. It was worth the indignity.",
      pyromancer: "Static crackled over everyone, again and again, and my hair stood straight up! I stood near the healers and let them mend me while I kept the fire coming.",
      thermist: "Static on everyone. Cold: gather in, near the healers. Then it built and built, hot and relentless, and we held together.",
      driftblade: "Static on all of us and biting, again and again, so I stopped running for once and stayed near the healers and let them mend me through it.",
      marionettist: "The whole company charged with static, stinging repeatedly. Blocking: everyone downstage near the healers. (The Doll endured it stoically.)",
      cleric: "Static settled on everyone and stung again and again. I gathered the faithful close and mended them all as it went, never falling behind. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The storm gathers like weather building over the valley. I planted in all my lot the moment the static settled, so the mending grew as the stings came. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "Static, again and again, a steady beat on everyone. I mend on every beat and never fall behind, because the Thunderhead lands right after. I'll have kept time.",
      alembist: "Ailment: static on everyone, stinging repeatedly, then the Thunderhead. Remedy: everyone close, mending poured continuously. Note: shelf empty by the end. Worth it.",
      threadweaver: "Static on every stitch, again and again. I tied them all together, close, so nobody took the stinging alone. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Thunderhead': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The gathered static broke as one great blast on all of us. I'd kept my guard for that moment, and the healers had the line whole first. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The static broke all at once over every one of us. I had saved my strength for it and the healers had us full beforehand. Being whole is worth a great deal.",
      thresher: "All that static came due at once and broke on every one of us. I knew it was ripe, so I had my guard up and came in mended beforehand. Nothing to step out of; only to be ready.",
      bloodreaver: "The Thunderhead broke on all of us. A fine scar! I took it with everything I had left and drank back what I could.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the static's ticking ceases. The answer: every ward raised, the wounded mended first, and then the Thunderhead breaks over us. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The final course: a Thunderhead served to everyone at once. One eats it whole, with every bit of fortitude one brought to table. Heavy, but I was hungry.",
      oathbroker: "The Thunderhead is the storm's balloon payment, due from all the undersigned at once. We met it whole, every guard raised. Countersigned.",
      ironpalm: "The Thunderhead spoke. We had raised our guards and breathed first. It passed.",
      ranger: "Thunderhead broke on all of us. Guard up, mended, kept shooting.",
      shadowblade: "The Thunderhead broke on all of us at once. I had my little tricks ready to soften it, and so did everyone else. It never saw me flinch.",
      pyromancer: "The whole sky collapsed on us in one great boom! I'd saved my best protection for exactly that moment. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "The Thunderhead. Cold: guard up, mended, ready. Then the heat, all of it, at once.",
      driftblade: "The Thunderhead broke over all of us and I'd saved my best guard for it and was mended, and then it was over and I could run again.",
      marionettist: "The scene's finale: a Thunderhead on the whole house. The company met it whole, every protection raised. (Standing ovation.)",
      cleric: "The Thunderhead broke over everyone. I had the faithful whole before it landed, and each had raised their own guard. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "The Thunderhead breaks like a summer storm finally letting go. My lot were mended and guarded before it. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "The Thunderhead lands at the end of the static, on the downbeat. Everyone whole, every guard raised. I'll have undone one, at most.",
      alembist: "Ailment: one great blast after the static. Remedy: everyone topped up, every guard raised. Note: brewed well in advance.",
      threadweaver: "The Thunderhead broke over the whole hem. I had every stitch mended and tight beforehand. Nothing snapped.",
    } },
    'Skyfall': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He called the sky down on the spot where one of us stood. The nearer you are, the worse it falls. I gave ground away from it, as far as the edge allowed. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "He dropped the sky on the spot where one of us had been. The closer you are, the harder it lands. I went as far from it as the summit allowed.",
      thresher: "The weather picked the spot where one of us had stood and brought the sky down on it. The nearer you stand, the harder it falls, so I walked off to the far edge of the field and let it land on empty soil.",
      bloodreaver: "The big one called the sky down where one of us stood. Got as far from that spot as I could. Even I don't want that one.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the clouds open over the spot where one of us stood as he called it. The answer: everyone as far from that spot as the summit permits. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The sky itself, served on one spot, the plate where a guest had stood. The closer you sit, the larger the portion. I sat at the far end of the table.",
      oathbroker: "The sky falls on a set spot, with liability by distance: the nearer, the more owed. We stood as far away as the summit allowed.",
      ironpalm: "The sky fell where one of us had stood. I walked as far from it as the edge allowed. It spoke softly at that distance.",
      ranger: "Sky came down where one of us had stood. Went to the far side, as far as the edge let me. Barely felt it.",
      shadowblade: "The sky fell on the spot where one of us stood when he called it. I withdrew to the far side of the summit, as one does from an unpleasant guest.",
      pyromancer: "The sky itself came down on one spot, brighter than anything I can do! I ran as far from it as the edge allowed. Eyebrows intact.",
      thermist: "Sky, falling on one spot. Cold: the farthest point from it. Then the blast, hot even at the edge.",
      driftblade: "The sky came down where one of us had stood and I ran away from it, all the way to the edge, as far as the summit would let me.",
      marionettist: "The sky falls on the mark where a player stood. The company exits to the farthest wings. (The Doll and I were, naturally, already there.)",
      cleric: "The sky came down on the spot where one of the faithful had stood. I sent everyone as far from it as the summit allowed. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The sky came down like hail on one field. My lot went to the farthest corner of the farm. Distance is the only shelter from that.",
      chronomancer: "He calls it on where someone stands, and it falls a few beats later. Go as far from that spot as you can. I'll have been there before.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the sky falling on one spot. Remedy: distance, as much as possible. Note: the nearer, the worse.",
      threadweaver: "The sky fell on where one of my stitches had stood. I pulled everyone to the far edge, all together.",
    } },
    'Heavens Fall': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The sky came down on a marked spot beside him. All of us stood in it together, gold and blue both, and held it up as a line. Each fall heavier than the last. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Under the falling sky a circle was painted at his side. We crowded into it, mixed colours, and split the load. Each blow weighed more than the one before.",
      thresher: "A patch was drawn at his side, and the sky meant to land on it. Every one of us crowded in, mixed colours, shoulder to shoulder like sheaves, and shared the weight. It got heavier each time.",
      bloodreaver: "The sky came down and all of us stood under it together, gold and blue mixed. Every fall a little heavier. Better and better!",
      runeguard: "The omen: a column of sky marked on the floor beside the Titan. The answer: the whole party within it, both charges present, sharing the blow. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The final course, shared by all at one table: the sky itself. Everyone in the circle, gold and blue both, splitting the plate. Richer each time.",
      oathbroker: "The sky is a joint debt, payable by all the undersigned together, and both charges must sign. We stood within it as one. Countersigned.",
      ironpalm: "The sky came down. We all stood in it together, both colours. We held it up. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Sky marked a spot by it. All of us in, both colours. Shared it.",
      shadowblade: "The sky came down on a marked spot, and we all crowded into it together, gold and blue. Sharing is usually beneath me, but I made an exception.",
      pyromancer: "The sky itself! All of us stood in the spot together, gold and blue, and held it up between us! Each one heavier. Somewhat singed, but triumphant.",
      thermist: "Sky, marked spot. Cold: everyone in, both colours. Hot: it lands, we hold, again and again.",
      driftblade: "The sky came down on a marked spot and all of us had to land in it together, both colours, and I did, every time, and hated it a little less each time.",
      marionettist: "The finale: the whole company onstage together, beneath the falling sky, gold and blue. (The Doll held her corner.)",
      cleric: "The sky came down on a marked spot. I gathered every one of the faithful into it, gold and blue together. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The sky came down on one marked field. My lot gathered in it, gold and blue together, and held it up.",
      chronomancer: "The sky comes down on a marked spot, again and again, each one heavier. All of us in it, both colours, every beat.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the sky on a marked spot. Remedy: everyone in, both colours. Note: heavier each time.",
      threadweaver: "The sky came down on a marked spot. I pulled every stitch into it, gold and blue together. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Grounding Rods': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the harder climb an iron rod hung over each gold one of us and was driven in wherever they stood. Ours went a stride either side of Volkar, one east, one west, level with him. I watched them plant and went to my partner's. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Hard climb: every gold one of us had an iron rod hanging above, planted wherever they happened to stand. Ours went in east and west of Volkar, level with him, and the blue ones came to join them. I went to my partner's as soon as it was in.",
      thresher: "Harder climb: a rod hangs over every gold one of us and goes into the floor wherever it stands. So we stood the golds a stride out either side of Volkar, level with him, east and west, and the blues wandered over to meet them. Tidy fence.",
      bloodreaver: "Iron rods over the two golds, hammered in where they stood. A stride off the big one, one east, one west, square across from each other through him. I found my partner's rod and stayed by it. No scar. Pity.",
      runeguard: "The omen: an iron rod over each gold charge. The answer: plant them a stride to either side of the Titan, east and west, in the very row he stands in; the blue partners mark where they fall and join them. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host handed each gold guest an iron skewer, planted wherever they stood. Ours went a stride to either side of him, east and west, level with his middle, like cutlery laid properly. I joined my partner at hers. Correctly set, for once.",
      oathbroker: "Each gold signatory is issued a rod, fixed wherever they stand until the fronts have passed, so placement is everything. One a stride east of him, one a stride west, in his own row. The blues countersign at their partner's rod. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Rods hung over the golds. Where they stood, the rods stayed. One a stride east of the mountain, one a stride west, level. I went to my partner's rod. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Rods over the two golds. Planted a stride off the stag's flanks, east and west, dead level with it. Partner came to mine. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "An iron rod hung over me, gold that night, and stayed wherever I stood. I planted it a stride off the mark's west flank; the other gold took the east, both level with him. My partner found me there. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "An iron rod over my head! It drives in wherever you stand, so I planted mine a stride off Volkar's east flank, and the other gold took his west, level with him. My blue partner came to me. Very elegant. Unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: rods over the golds. They stay where planted. One a stride east of him, one a stride west, same row. Blue: watch them land, then go to your partner's. The heat comes later; this part runs cold.",
      driftblade: "A rod hung over my partner and she had to stand still to plant it, which I couldn't bear to watch, a stride off the giant's west side, the other one east, level with him, and I swung in through to her rod.",
      marionettist: "Props: an iron rod over each gold player, set wherever they stand. Blocking: a stride either side of our leading man, one east, one west, level with him. Blue partners join theirs. (The Doll admires good set placement.)",
      cleric: "Hard climb: a rod hung over every gold one of us and was driven in wherever they stood, so we chose our footing with care. A stride out, east and west of Volkar, level with him; the blues went to their partners. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Rods over the golds, driven in like fence posts wherever they stood. My lot set them a stride either side of Old Thunder, east and west, in the row through him. Each blue went to their partner's post. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "The rods come down over the golds, and where they stand they stay until the storm has passed. A stride east of Volkar, a stride west, level with him. The blues join a beat later. I'll have known it mattered; it does.",
      alembist: "Ailment: an iron rod over each gold, planted where they stand, fixed until the fronts pass. Remedy: a stride to either side of him, east and west, in his own row. Note: the blues go to their partner's rod. Placement is the whole recipe.",
      threadweaver: "Rods hung over my two golds, so I placed them like pins: a stride off either side of the old windbag, east and west, level with him. Each blue stitched to their partner's rod. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Supercell': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Three squall fronts, always the same order: west, then north, then east. Each stops at a rod and leaves still air past it. My partner and I went round our rod, never leaving Volkar. Then the cell broke over all of us; we came in mended. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Wind fronts came in a fixed sequence, I learned: from the west, then the north, then the east. Each died against a rod, with calm beyond. We circled our own rod with Volkar always in reach, and stood whole when the cell finally let go.",
      thresher: "Squall fronts come in a set order, west then north then east, each ending at a rod with still air behind it. My partner and I walked round our post, east side, south side, west side, staying on Volkar, and came in mended for the cloudburst.",
      bloodreaver: "Walls of lightning rolling over the summit: west, then north, then east. A rod stops them, and past it the ground stays dry and still. Round our rod we went, east, south, west, still hitting the big one. Then the whole cell burst over everyone. A fine scar, at last!",
      runeguard: "The omen: three fronts, ever in one order, west, north, east, a lane of still air past each rod. The answer: circle one's rod with one's partner, east side, then south, then west; and be whole as the last passes, for the cell then breaks over us all. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host served three storms in a row, from the west, the north, the east, and each rod kept a dry seat behind it. My partner and I moved round ours, east, south, west, eating at his elbow throughout. Then dessert: the whole cell, served to every guest at once. Heavy.",
      oathbroker: "Three fronts in fixed order, west, north, east; each rod exempts the ground beyond it. The undersigned went round their rod, east side, south side, west side, never leaving the counterparty. The fine print: as the last front passes, the cell breaks on all of us, owed in common. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The storm came from the west, the north, the east. The rods quieted the air behind them. I walked round ours with my partner: east, south, west. Then the storm spoke to all of us at once. We were ready. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Fronts from the west, north, east, in that order. Rod stops each; still lane past it. Round the rod: east side, south, west. Never left the stag. Last front out, the cell breaks on everyone. Came in mended.",
      shadowblade: "Three fronts swept the summit, west, north, east, ever in that order, and each rod left a lane of still air beyond it. My partner and I circled ours, east, south, west, still at the mark's side. Then the cell broke over everyone, quite vulgar. I was mended. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Three storm fronts, every one brighter than me, rude! West, then north, then east, and a still lane past each rod. I went round mine with my partner, east, south, west, never stopped casting. Then the whole cell burst over all of us. Hair: upright. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: west, north, east; the still lane past each rod. East side, south side, west side. Hot: the fronts overlapping, the rush round the rod, my partner at my elbow. Then the cell broke over everyone and I ran scalding. Whole beforehand, or too far gone.",
      driftblade: "Fronts came through, west then north then east, each starting before the last had gone, and I ran round our rod with my partner through the still lanes, east side, south side, west side, never leaving the giant, and then the cell broke over all of us and there was nowhere to run.",
      marionettist: "A three-scene storm, always in order: west, north, east. Blocking: each pair circles its rod, east side, south side, west side, in the still lane, never leaving our leading man. Finale: the cell breaks over the whole house. (The company, mended in advance, took it bravely.)",
      cleric: "Three fronts crossed the summit, from the west, then the north, then the east. Each pair went round their rod, east side, south side, west side, staying by Volkar. As the last one passed, the cell broke over everyone; I had them mended before it. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Supercell rolls in like three weathers back to back: a west wind, a north wind, an east wind. My lot sheltered in the lee of their rods, east side, south side, west side, never far from Old Thunder. Then the cloudburst over all of us. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "West, north, east, each front starting before the last has passed, the same three bars every time. Round the rod: east side, south side, west side, a beat early each. Then the cell breaks on everyone, on the last beat; I'll have mended them already.",
      alembist: "Ailment: three fronts, west, north, east, overlapping. Remedy: each pair round their rod, east side, south side, west side; the lane past it stays dry. Note: the cell then bursts over everyone; dose the whole party before the last front passes.",
      threadweaver: "Fronts from the west, the north, the east, one on another's heels. I kept each pair tight round their rod: east side, south side, west side, never leaving the old windbag. Then the cell broke over the whole hem, and I'd mended every stitch first. Mind the slack.",
    } },
  },
};
